Title: after-merge run: wake the landing session, alert the author on red
Created: 2026-11-12
Author: reviewer

--- log ---
2026-11-12T13:36Z reviewer created

--- body ---
## Context

The after-merge runner (`merge-gate.py run-after`, started detached by
hooks/reference-transaction) tells no one when it finishes: the result sits in
`<git-common-dir>/idh/after-merge.log` and the gate note until a session reads
them. Author's choice 2026-11-12 (options A+B of the guichet's Imagine): the
landing session is woken on the result, and the author is alerted on red.

Measured over 630 after-merge records since 2026-10-27: the check set takes
median ~50 s, p99 ~100 s, max 146 s (plus a few seconds of worktree setup);
a tip queued behind a busy runner waits at most one more run. 66 of 630 were
red.

## Ask

- A: `merge-gate.py wait-after --tip <sha>` polls the gate notes until the tip
  carries an after-merge record, bounded (default 600 s, twice the worst
  observed wait), and exits on its result. `raid_land.py` prints the
  `runtime.py wake arm` line that runs it after a gated landing; the merge
  skill's step 9 tells the lander to arm it. Waking reuses the existing wake
  backends (Claude background command, Codex timer, Pi inbox); no new transport.
- B: on a red record, the runner calls `runtime.py notify` (desktop popup
  plus alert file, rate limited per tip). Best effort: a failed notify
  changes nothing.
- Red stays one owner's: no broadcast to other sessions (rules/workflow.md,
  a red gate on main is a broadcast).

## Exit criteria
- [ ] `wait-after` exits 0 on a pass record, 1 on a fail record, 2 at the bound, 5 on an environment record
- [ ] A red after-merge run writes an alert for its tip; a green run writes none
- [ ] raid_land prints the wake-arm line after a gated landing
- [ ] skills/merge step 9 says to arm it
